      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You can argue that relying on UBI as a cure-all for automation ignores the immediate reality that job displacement happens faster than policy implementation can provide capital. Proponents often assume future technology will generate enough profit to fund the program, yet history shows governments consistently lag behind technological transformations, leaving workers destitute in the interim. To dismantle this, students should highlight that automation requires active retraining, hiring adjustments, and safety nets that have actually been eroded in recent decades. By focusing on the lack of infrastructure to transition workers rather than just handing out cash, you expose the unrealistic optimism behind the \"automation-proof\" claim.",
